@startuml
title Local-First Conflict Handling with ~--resync
start

:Begin local-first reconciliation with ~--resync;
note right
  Operating mode:
  onedrive ~--sync ~--local-first ~--resync

  Two behaviours interact:
  - ~--local-first asks the client to evaluate local intent first
  - ~--resync discards the previous last-applied DB baseline

  Therefore same-path differing content must be handled conservatively;
  the client has no historical DB state with which to prove which side
  changed after the previous successful synchronisation.
end note

:Discard previous local sync database state;
:Initialise required online root / drive identities;
:Scan local filesystem before normal online reconciliation;

repeat
  :Take next local file candidate;
  :Check whether same logical target already exists online;
  if (No online item exists?) then (yes)
    :Upload as new local file;
  else (no)
    :Compare local content with online content;
    if (Content matches?) then (yes)
      :Bind online identity / metadata to rebuilt DB state;
      note right
        Identical bytes need no safeBackup. The database identity can be
        rebuilt directly because preserving another copy would add no value.
      end note
    else (no)
      note right
        There is no trusted pre-resync baseline for this pathname.
        Different local and online content therefore represents an
        ambiguous conflict that requires conservative handling.
      end note
      :Evaluate local versus current online state;
      if (Local version is selected for upload?) then (yes)
        :Upload as modified content;
      else (online conflict)
        :Create and verify independent safeBackup copy;
        note right
          LOCAL DATA LOSS PREVENTION

          Preserve the differing local bytes before the online canonical
          version is allowed to remain authoritative for this pathname.
        end note
        :Upload safeBackup as a new file;
        if (Not ~--upload-only?) then (yes)
          :Apply current online canonical file transactionally;
          note right
            Bidirectional result:
            - local content survives as safeBackup
            - current online canonical becomes canonical locally
          end note
        else (upload-only)
          :Retain local canonical path after preservation;
        endif
      endif
    endif
  endif
repeat while (More local candidates?) is (yes)

:Reconcile remaining Microsoft OneDrive online state;
stop
@enduml
